Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.989 --> 00:00:05.950
The next module, os, allows a programmer to
work within the operating system to perform

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:08.290
actions in Python.

00:00:08.290 --> 00:00:14.090
As with the io module, os needs to be imported
to have its functions available.

00:00:14.090 --> 00:00:19.130
One such function is to rename a file, which
may be needed when playing a game so that

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:25.610
a new file, such as a configuration or description
file on a game character, can be written.

00:00:25.610 --> 00:00:32.790
In this code example, 612-os, we have code
that will show our current working directory,

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:35.649
and a type of file within the directory.

00:00:35.649 --> 00:00:40.360
From a gaming or other aspect, you can use
this code to where files are being written,

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:48.020
and if a specific file exists, it can then
be overwritten, appended to, renamed, or deleted.

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:51.630
On line 1 of this code, we import the os module.

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:59.219
Then, on line three, a print statement uses
the os module and the getcwd function to get

00:00:59.219 --> 00:01:03.789
the current working directory.

00:01:03.789 --> 00:01:11.800
Line 5 starts a for loop that uses the listdir
method, which lists all the files within a

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:13.930
directory.

00:01:13.930 --> 00:01:20.640
We see an if statement, inside the loop, on
lines 6 and 7, that prints any file name that

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.490
ends with .txt.

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:34.570
I will run the code, and we see our working
directory plus a few text files in the directory.

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:40.390
For renaming a file, like adding the word
old to old files, that should not be in use,

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:45.110
we can use the rename method in the os module.

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:59.799
I will add code, on line 9, to rename the
601-message.txt file OLD601-message.txt.

00:01:59.799 --> 00:02:13.470
Then, I will cut this for loop and paste it
after the rename code so that we can see the

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:16.430
results once we run the code.

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:23.960
I will run the code, and we see the list of
text files, including the one we have renamed.

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:27.459
Before we move on, it's important to highlight
some other versatile functionalities that

00:02:27.459 --> 00:02:29.640
the os module offers.

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:34.489
For instance, we’ll cover the os.path submodule
in the next video.

00:02:34.489 --> 00:02:39.849
The os.environ dictionary lets you access
and manipulate environment variables, which

00:02:39.849 --> 00:02:45.489
can be particularly useful for setting up
dynamic application configurations.

00:02:45.489 --> 00:02:49.409
The module also has the capability to run
shell commands within Python code through

00:02:49.409 --> 00:02:51.709
os.system().

00:02:51.709 --> 00:02:58.150
For those dealing with directory operations,
you have a variety of methods such as os.mkdir(),

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:07.080
os.rmdir(), and os.makedirs() for creating
and deleting directories.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:12.780
If you need in-depth file information like
size, permissions, or creation time, the os.stat()

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:15.159
function provides these details.

00:03:15.159 --> 00:03:20.310
For temporary data storage or intermediate
processing steps, the module also offers utilities

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:22.980
for working with temporary files and directories.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:28.030
The os module helps us manipulate files from
within a Python app, helping us avoid using

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:32.129
another source to modify those files and thus
complicate a system that works with files.

